    `With Dasharatha's death confirmed, the royal household prepared to perform the king's final rites. The palace remained filled with mourning, but the duties owed to the departed king could not be postponed. The ministers and priests arranged the necessary ceremonies, while Bharata and Shatrughna prepared to stand beside their father's body. Bharata moved through each task with a heavy heart, feeling the absence of Rama at every stage.`,

    `As Bharata approached his father, the full weight of the loss returned to him. He had not been present during Dasharatha's final hours. He had not heard his father's last words or been able to hold his hand. He knelt beside him and bowed his head. "Father," he whispered, "I have come home, but I am too late to see you. I was not here when you needed your sons."`,

    `Shatrughna stood close beside him. He, too, had lost the chance to speak to his father one final time. For a while, neither brother moved. They had been raised in the palace, surrounded by teachers, attendants and the traditions of their royal family, but none of those things could prepare them for the silence of a parent who would never answer them again.`,

    `Bharata's thoughts returned to Rama. He imagined how his eldest brother would have stood beside their father, how carefully he would have performed the duties required of a son and how deeply he would have mourned the king. The thought made Bharata's grief more painful. Rama was the son Dasharatha had longed to see, yet he remained far away, unable to attend the final rites.`,

    `The priests guided Bharata through the funeral observances. He listened carefully and performed the duties expected of him, trying to give his father the honour he deserved. His hands moved according to the instructions he received, but his thoughts repeatedly returned to the same questions. Could this have been prevented? Would his father still be alive if Rama had remained in Ayodhya? He knew there was no answer that could restore the dead, yet he could not stop asking.`,

    `When the rites were completed, Bharata remained quiet for a long time. The people around him offered words of consolation, but he found little comfort in them. A kingdom could appoint ministers, perform ceremonies and continue its daily work, but a son could not replace the father he had lost. Dasharatha's absence had changed the meaning of every familiar place in the palace.`,

    `Shatrughna came to stand beside him. "We must return to the palace," he said gently. Bharata looked once more toward the place where his father's final rites had been performed. "I know," he answered. "But I do not know how to return to the life we had before." Shatrughna did not pretend that he did. He simply remained beside his brother as they walked away together.`,

    `The rites had fulfilled their sacred purpose, but they had not brought peace to Bharata. His father was gone, Rama was still in exile, and the succession remained unresolved in his heart. He understood that his next decision would affect the kingdom, but before he could think as a prince, he needed to act as a son and a brother.`
