    `Once Bharata had made his decision, preparations began for the journey to Chitrakuta. Ministers, elders, priests and members of the royal household would accompany him, while attendants arranged the provisions needed for the road. Bharata did not want the journey to resemble a coronation procession. He was not travelling to display authority or claim a kingdom. He was going to find his brother and ask him to return.`,

    `Before leaving, Bharata visited the queens. Kausalya received him with eyes still swollen from mourning. When he bowed before her, she placed her hands upon his head and tried to speak, but emotion interrupted her. Bharata looked up at her. "Mother, I know I cannot undo what has happened. I cannot bring Father back, and I cannot erase the sorrow Rama has endured. But I will go to him. I will not accept the throne while he remains away."`,

    `Kausalya drew him closer. "Bharata, you must not carry the blame for every decision made in your absence." He shook his head. "I know that I did not ask for this, Mother. But I cannot behave as though it has nothing to do with me. My name was used to justify Rama's exile, and I must go to him myself. I need him to hear from me that I never desired his place."`,

    `Sumitra also offered her blessing. Her thoughts naturally turned toward Lakshmana, who had accompanied Rama into the forest. "When you see your brothers," she said, "do not let the sorrow of this journey become another reason for anger within the family. They have gone through enough." Bharata bowed deeply. "I shall remember your words, Mother. I want to bring our family together, not deepen the divisions between us."`,

    `Before the procession departed, Bharata looked back toward the palace. The place where he had grown up now held memories of his father's death, his mother's decision and the crown he had refused. Shatrughna came to stand beside him. "Are you ready?" he asked. Bharata took a slow breath. "No," he admitted. "I do not know what Rama will say when he sees me. But I cannot remain here without trying."`,

    `Shatrughna nodded. "Then we shall go together." Bharata looked at him gratefully. He had been carrying his guilt and grief almost entirely within himself, and Shatrughna's presence reminded him that he did not have to make the journey alone. They mounted their chariots and began travelling toward the forest.`,

    `As the city gates receded behind them, Bharata thought about the last time Rama had left Ayodhya. The people had followed him, unable to bear the separation. Now Bharata was travelling in the opposite spirit: not to carry Rama away, but to bring him home. Yet he knew that love alone might not persuade his brother to abandon the vow he had accepted.`,

    `The road stretched ahead, leading toward the forests and the mountain country where Rama had settled. Bharata carried no joy at the thought of reaching his destination. He carried a request, an apology and a hope that he could not yet name with certainty. Whatever answer Rama gave him, Bharata knew he must first stand before his brother and speak honestly.`
