    `The news of Dasharatha's death continued to weigh upon Rama. Though he had accepted exile with a composed heart, he had never imagined that his father would die before they could meet again. He sat quietly for a long while, looking beyond the hermitage toward the trees. The forest around him had not changed, yet it seemed that the place itself had grown more distant from the life he had once known.`,

    `Bharata sat nearby, unwilling to leave his brother alone in his grief. At last, Rama spoke. "When I left Ayodhya, I believed that Father would endure the separation. I knew it would cause him pain, but I did not imagine that the sorrow would take him from us."`,

    `Bharata replied, "He could not stop thinking about you. He regretted the promise that had brought you away, but he could not find a way to undo it without breaking his word." Rama lowered his eyes. "He was caught between the promise he had made and the love he bore his son. I wish I could have spared him that burden."`,

    `Lakshmana moved closer. "Brother, you did not cause Father's death. You honoured his command because you believed it was right. Do not let grief turn your love for him into blame against yourself." Rama looked at him with quiet sorrow. "I know what you say, Lakshmana. Yet a son cannot hear that his father died longing for him and feel no pain that he was absent."`,

    `Sita sat beside Rama. "Your father knew your heart," she said softly. "He knew that you did not leave because you loved him less. You left because you believed that his word must be honoured." Rama listened, but the thought of Dasharatha's final hours remained painful. He remembered the king's face when the exile had been announced and the struggle he had witnessed between a father's love and a ruler's promise.`,

    `Bharata moved nearer. "If you had returned, Father would have been comforted. I know that. But I also know that you would never have wanted his word to become meaningless. I do not know how to reconcile these things. I only know that I want to bring you home."`,

    `Rama placed a hand upon Bharata's shoulder. "Your love is clear to me. I do not doubt that you want what is best for Ayodhya. But we cannot allow our sorrow to decide that a promise should be forgotten whenever it becomes difficult. If we do, what meaning will our word have when others depend upon it?"`,

    `Bharata absorbed the answer in silence. He did not agree that Rama must remain in the forest, but he understood that his brother was not refusing him out of pride or indifference. Rama was trying to remain faithful to a principle that had guided his life long before this crisis.`,

    `As the conversation ended, the brothers remained together in the hermitage. Their grief did not disappear, nor did their disagreement. But they had spoken honestly, without suspicion or accusation. Bharata had finally been able to tell Rama the truth, and Rama had received it as a brother who loved him, even though he could not grant the request that had brought him to Chitrakuta.`
