    `After learning of Dasharatha's death, Rama knew that he must perform the rites appropriate to a son mourning his father. Though he was far from Ayodhya, he would not allow distance to keep him from fulfilling the duties that remained possible to him. He went with Sita, Lakshmana and Bharata toward the Mandakini River, carrying the sorrow of a son who had not been present when his father died.`,

    `The river moved quietly through the forest, its surface catching the morning light. Rama stood at its bank for a moment, gathering himself before beginning the observances. Bharata remained close beside him, feeling the pain of watching his brother perform these rites in exile when the royal family should have been together in Ayodhya.`,

    `Rama bowed his head and spoke softly. "Father, I was not beside you when you left this world. I could not hold your hand or hear your final words. Accept what I am able to offer here, far from the palace you ruled and the family you loved." His voice trembled, but he continued with the care and reverence the occasion demanded.`,

    `Lakshmana stood silently nearby, his eyes lowered. He had spent much of the journey into exile trying to protect Rama from every hardship, but this was a sorrow he could not prevent or repair. Sita remained respectfully beside the brothers, sharing their grief without intruding upon the solemnity of the rites.`,

    `Bharata watched Rama closely. The elder prince performed every act with patience, as though the duties themselves gave shape to feelings that words could not contain. Bharata had hoped to bring Rama back to Ayodhya, but he now saw that Rama's commitment to duty was not something he invoked only when it was convenient. Even in exile, he continued to honour the responsibilities he believed belonged to him.`,

    `When the observances were complete, Rama remained beside the river. He looked across the water, thinking of Dasharatha and the palace that now stood without him. "May Father find peace," he said quietly. "May the sorrow that surrounded his final days no longer trouble him."`,

    `Bharata lowered his head in prayer. He had been unable to serve his father during his final hours, but he could at least stand beside his brother now. The two princes remained together in silence, united by their love for the man they had lost.`,

    `As they returned toward the hermitage, the forest seemed unchanged, but the family had been altered forever. Dasharatha could not be brought back, and the grief of his sons could not be undone. What remained was the question of how they would honour him: Bharata by refusing a throne he believed belonged to Rama, and Rama by continuing to uphold the promise he had accepted.`
